1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,680
 In this video, I'll be discussing loops.

2
00:00:04,680 --> 00:00:11,580
 We'll cover basic loops, while loops, and infinite loops.

3
00:00:11,580 --> 00:00:15,280
 So when you're writing programs, it's often required to repeat actions in code more than

4
00:00:15,280 --> 00:00:18,720
 once, or to iterate over the items in a collection.

5
00:00:18,720 --> 00:00:21,620
 And these tests are accomplished by using loops.

6
00:00:21,620 --> 00:00:29,940
 In Go, there's only one keyword that we use for looping, and that is the "for" keyword.

7
00:00:29,940 --> 00:00:32,560
 Let's take a look at the basic C-style loop.

8
00:00:32,560 --> 00:00:38,440
 So we have the "for" keyword, followed by the initialization statement, a condition check,

9
00:00:38,440 --> 00:00:42,120
 and the "post loop" statement.

10
00:00:42,120 --> 00:00:47,520
 Each one of these portions is separated with semicolons, so you have three sections.

11
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:51,200
 Initialization, condition, and post.

12
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:54,560
 To recreate our loop with the "for" keyword, in our initialization statement, we would

13
00:00:54,560 --> 00:00:59,120
 typically create a new variable, and it's usually named "I" for iteration.

14
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:02,500
 Usually you start at zero, but you can start at any point you want.

15
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:09,840
 We're using the Create and Assign operator to create "I" and assign it the value of zero.

16
00:01:09,840 --> 00:01:13,600
 Our condition portion of the loop will be checked before every iteration.

17
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:16,880
 So what's going to happen is we'll create this variable "I".

18
00:01:16,880 --> 00:01:19,680
 It's then going to be checked to see if it's less than 10.

19
00:01:19,680 --> 00:01:22,960
 And then if it is, we're going to enter into this loop.

20
00:01:22,960 --> 00:01:25,720
 So then all the code in here will get executed.

21
00:01:25,720 --> 00:01:30,160
 Once execution completes, we'll go back up to this "post" statement, and "I" will be

22
00:01:30,160 --> 00:01:34,520
 incremented by using the "plus-plus" operator.

23
00:01:34,520 --> 00:01:36,600
 "plus-plus" just adds one to "I".

24
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:41,800
 So in the first iteration, "I" will be zero, "I" is less than 10, because it's zero.

25
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:47,880
 The code will execute, then "I" will be incremented by one, and then we'll check again.

26
00:01:47,880 --> 00:01:53,200
 So is "I" less than 10, it'll be one at that point, yes it is, and we'll execute.

27
00:01:53,200 --> 00:01:56,560
 And this will continue until finally "I" is equal to 10.

28
00:01:56,560 --> 00:02:01,320
 Once it's 10, this check will fail, and it'll be false, and then we'll exit out of

29
00:02:01,320 --> 00:02:07,320
 the loop, and continue executing code.

30
00:02:07,320 --> 00:02:11,560
 Go does support while loops, however, we can only use the "for" keyword in Go, since

31
00:02:11,560 --> 00:02:13,200
 there's only one loop in keyword.

32
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:18,960
 So to create a while loop, we'll just use the "for" keyword, and a condition.

33
00:02:18,960 --> 00:02:23,000
 Note that the condition must have the variable created beforehand, so "I" needs to

34
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:25,680
 exist prior to using this loop.

35
00:02:25,680 --> 00:02:30,200
 If this happens to evaluate the true, then the loop will execute.

36
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:34,600
 One thing to note with this style of looping is you'll always want to adjust the loop

37
00:02:34,600 --> 00:02:43,240
 counter within this loop, otherwise it'll just go on forever.

38
00:02:43,240 --> 00:02:45,600
 We also have support for infinite loops.

39
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:49,640
 So if we want an infinite loop, we can just use "for", and then nothing else.

40
00:02:49,640 --> 00:02:53,880
 All we need is our curly braces right here, and then the code within is just going to execute

41
00:02:53,880 --> 00:02:55,880
 over and over and over.

42
00:02:55,880 --> 00:03:00,160
 In order to exit an infinite loop, we'll have to use the "break" keyword.

43
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:05,600
 Usually you would check, so in this case we're checking if something happened is true,

44
00:03:05,600 --> 00:03:07,280
 then we'll break.

45
00:03:07,280 --> 00:03:11,400
 And if it's false, then we'll just keep continuing on forever, since this is an infinite

46
00:03:11,400 --> 00:03:14,440
 loop.

47
00:03:14,440 --> 00:03:17,640
 Loops like this will be used for something like web servers, or if you're reading data

48
00:03:17,640 --> 00:03:25,240
 from some kind of data stream, and then you would choose the condition on which to break.

49
00:03:25,240 --> 00:03:30,280
 We also have the "Continue" keyword, which can be used to skip the current loop.

50
00:03:30,280 --> 00:03:34,440
 So we have a typical for loop, where we're setting i to 0, as long as it's less than 10,

51
00:03:34,440 --> 00:03:38,960
 we're going to loop, and we're incrementing one time each iteration.

52
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:43,120
 Within our loop we have an if statement, and we're checking the i with remainder division

53
00:03:43,120 --> 00:03:46,160
 of 2, and if that's equal to 0, we'll continue.

54
00:03:46,160 --> 00:03:51,480
 So what this will do is this will skip every iteration where i is an even number.

55
00:03:51,480 --> 00:03:56,720
 That means as soon as "Continue" is hit, we'll go straight up here, and we'll do the

56
00:03:56,720 --> 00:03:58,280
 condition check again.

57
00:03:58,280 --> 00:04:03,160
 So this code will never get executed down here, if the "Continue" is hit first.

58
00:04:03,160 --> 00:04:08,200
 However, if we have an odd number, then this will be false, and "Continue" will not

59
00:04:08,200 --> 00:04:16,080
 be executed, and therefore we will print out the odd numbers.

60
00:04:16,080 --> 00:04:17,080
 Let's recap.

61
00:04:17,080 --> 00:04:19,040
 The "For" keyword creates a loop.

62
00:04:19,040 --> 00:04:23,160
 We can use the "Break" keyword to exit the loop on a specific condition.

63
00:04:23,160 --> 00:04:28,840
 The initialization variable can be used only within the loop block, and the post statement

64
00:04:28,840 --> 00:04:31,600
 executes every iteration.

65
00:04:31,600 --> 00:04:37,240
 We have three styles of loops, we have the typical loop, where you get to set the variable,

66
00:04:37,240 --> 00:04:40,160
 the condition, and how much it changes.

67
00:04:40,160 --> 00:04:45,760
 We have the while loop, where we have a variable created previously, and as long as

68
00:04:45,760 --> 00:04:50,640
 that variable is less than the value supplied here, then we'll execute the loop.

69
00:04:50,640 --> 00:04:55,400
 Always remember to increment that within your loop, otherwise it'll go on forever.

70
00:04:55,400 --> 00:04:58,560
 Then we have the intentional infinite loop, where there's nothing right here.

71
00:04:58,560 --> 00:05:02,160
 We just have four and our curly braces, and that will just go on forever.

